// bigmod — arithmetic in the ring of integers modulo n, which is the
// half of this package the crypto tier is here for.
//
// Every body is a `todo()`.  The signatures are the design, and the
// compiler checks them as claims; see the README for the stamp.
//
// THE LOAD-BEARING INTERFACE IS `BigMont`, AND IT IS A VALUE THE CALLER
// BUILDS ONCE PER MODULUS.  Montgomery form replaces a division by n
// with a multiply and a shift, which is what makes modular
// exponentiation affordable at all; the price is a setup — computing
// `n'`, the negated inverse of n modulo the limb base, and `R^2 mod n`
// for the conversion in — that costs more than a single reduction.  An
// API where every call re-derived it would be slower than one that
// never used Montgomery form.  So the setup is a value: `montgomery(n)`
// once, then `mont_mul`, `mont_sqr` and `mont_pow` against it as many
// times as the protocol needs.  An RSA signature verification is one
// context and 17 squarings; an RSA signature is one context and about
// 3000 operations; a caller that built the context inside the loop
// would pay the setup 3000 times and would never know why it was slow.
//
// THAT SHAPE IS WHY THIS PACKAGE IS WORTH HAVING AT `core` AT ALL.
// Every operation here is a pure function of values the caller holds:
// no allocation the caller cannot see, no clock, no entropy, no
// scratch state carried between calls.  A `core` package with a
// per-modulus context could have been a mutable handle with an `init`
// and a `free`; it is a value instead, and the whole budget follows
// from that one decision.
//
// CONSTANT TIME IS A CLAIM, AND IT IS MADE FUNCTION BY FUNCTION.  Three
// names carry `_ct`: `pow_ct`, `mont_pow_ct` and `select_ct`.  Those
// are the ones whose running time and memory access pattern do not
// depend on the secret they are given — a fixed square-and-multiply
// ladder that performs the multiply at every bit and discards it when
// the bit is 0, and a select that reads both arms.  Every OTHER
// function here is variable time and says so in its own comment: `pow`
// skips zero bits, `inverse` and `gcd` are extended Euclid and branch
// on the values, `reduce` divides.  A secret exponent goes through a
// `_ct` function or it leaks; a public one does not need to.
//
// AND THE CLAIM IS BOUNDED BY WHAT THIS LANGUAGE CAN PROMISE.  A
// constant-time claim in a high-level language is a claim about the
// algorithm, not about the machine: nothing here can stop a compiler
// from turning a branchless select back into a branch, and nothing here
// can keep a value out of a cache line.  The claim is that the
// ALGORITHM performs the same operations in the same order whatever the
// secret bits are, which is the part a library can be responsible for.
// The implementation lane owes a test that counts operations, and the
// README says so.

// ── plain modular arithmetic ────────────────────────────────────────

// `a mod n`, the least non-negative residue.
//
// `BigZeroModulus` when n is zero.  Variable time: it is a division.
//
// ```novo
// use bignum
// use bigfmt
// use bigmod
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match bignum.nat_from_int(17)
//         Ok(a) =>
//             match bignum.nat_from_int(5)
//                 Ok(n) =>
//                     match bigmod.reduce(a, n)
//                         Ok(r) => println(bigfmt.nat_to_decimal(r))   // 2
//                         Err(e) => println(e.message())
//                 Err(e) => println(e.message())
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn reduce(a: BigNat, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.reduce")

// A SIGNED number reduced into `0 ..= n - 1`.
//
// The floored remainder, not the truncated one, which is why it exists
// beside `reduce`: `of_num(-1, 7)` is 6 and not -1.  Written in terms
// of `bignum.floordivmod` for exactly that reason.
pub fn of_num(a: BigNum, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.of_num")

// `(a + b) mod n`, for `a` and `b` already reduced.
//
// A single conditional subtraction rather than a division, because both
// operands are below n — so this is cheap and the precondition is what
// makes it cheap.  An unreduced operand gives a wrong answer rather
// than a refusal: the function cannot tell, and checking would cost the
// division this call exists to avoid.
pub fn add(a: BigNat, b: BigNat, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.add")

// `(a - b) mod n`, for `a` and `b` already reduced.  A conditional
// addition; the same precondition as `add`.
pub fn sub(a: BigNat, b: BigNat, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.sub")

// `(a * b) mod n`.
//
// A full multiply and a division.  A caller doing this in a loop wants
// `mont_mul` against a `BigMont` instead, which is the same answer for
// a fraction of the cost once the context is built.
pub fn mul(a: BigNat, b: BigNat, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.mul")

// `(a * a) mod n`.  `bignum.nat_sqr` and a division.
pub fn sqr(a: BigNat, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.sqr")

// `(-a) mod n`, for `a` already reduced: `n - a`, and 0 for 0.
pub fn neg(a: BigNat, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.neg")

// `base^exponent mod n`, by square-and-multiply over the exponent's
// bits.
//
// VARIABLE TIME, and the variation is in the exponent: a set bit costs
// a multiply and a clear bit does not, so the running time reveals the
// exponent's Hamming weight and its length.  Fine for a public
// exponent — RSA's 65537, a Diffie-Hellman group check — and NOT fine
// for a private one.  `pow_ct` is the other one.
//
// `BigZeroModulus` for a zero modulus.
pub fn pow(base: BigNat, exponent: BigNat, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.pow")

// `base^exponent mod n` in CONSTANT TIME with respect to the exponent.
//
// A fixed ladder: every bit position performs a squaring and a
// multiplication, and `select_ct` chooses whether the multiplication's
// result or the untouched accumulator continues — so the operation
// sequence depends on the exponent's declared WIDTH and on nothing
// else.  `width` is that declared width in bits, and it is an argument
// rather than `bignum.bit_len(exponent)` precisely because the bit
// length of a secret is itself secret: an RSA private exponent is
// "2048 bits" by the key's size, not by where its top set bit happens
// to be.
//
// `BigWidthExceeded` when the exponent does not fit `width`.
//
// The modulus is not secret in any protocol this serves, so the
// reduction inside is the ordinary one.
pub fn pow_ct(base: BigNat, exponent: BigNat, width: Int, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.pow_ct")

// The modular inverse of `a` modulo `n`: the `x` in `0 ..= n - 1` with
// `a * x ≡ 1 (mod n)`.
//
// The binary extended Euclidean algorithm.  `BigNotInvertible` when `a`
// and `n` share a factor — which in an RSA key generator is not a
// failure but a fact worth having, because it means the candidate was
// not coprime with the totient and the next candidate is the answer.
//
// VARIABLE TIME: extended Euclid branches on the values it is reducing,
// so the running time depends on both operands.  There is no constant
// time inverse here, and the reason is that the crypto tier does not
// need one at this layer — an inverse modulo a PRIME is
// `pow_ct(a, n - 2, width, n)` by Fermat's little theorem, which is
// constant time already, and that is the call a curve implementation
// makes.  `bigmod.inverse` is for key generation and for CRT
// parameters, which run once and not against an attacker's clock.
//
// ```novo
// use bignum
// use bigfmt
// use bigmod
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match bignum.nat_from_int(3)
//         Ok(a) =>
//             match bignum.nat_from_int(11)
//                 Ok(n) =>
//                     match bigmod.inverse(a, n)
//                         Ok(x) => println(bigfmt.nat_to_decimal(x))   // 4
//                         Err(e) => println(e.message())
//                 Err(e) => println(e.message())
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn inverse(a: BigNat, n: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.inverse")

// The greatest common divisor of `a` and `n`.
//
// Here rather than only in `bignum` because it is what
// `BigNotInvertible` does not carry: a caller that met that refusal and
// wants the shared factor asks this.  Variable time.
pub fn gcd(a: BigNat, n: BigNat) -> BigNat []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.gcd")

// Whichever of `a` and `b` the `choose` bit selects — `b` when it is 1
// and `a` when it is 0 — reading BOTH and branching on neither.
//
// The primitive `pow_ct` is written on, published because every
// constant-time loop above this package needs the same thing and
// rewriting it per caller is how one of them ends up with an `if`.
//
// The two operands must already be the same limb length; a select
// between values of different lengths would leak the length, which is
// the one thing the caller is trying to hide.  `BigWidthExceeded` with
// both lengths when they differ.
pub fn select_ct(a: BigNat, b: BigNat, choose: Int) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.select_ct")

// Whether two reduced values are equal, in time that does not depend on
// where they first differ.
//
// The comparison a MAC check and a point-equality test both need.
// Variable in the operands' LENGTH, which is public, and constant in
// their contents.
pub fn eq_ct(a: BigNat, b: BigNat) -> Bool []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.eq_ct")

// ── Montgomery form ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// BigMont is everything about one modulus that a Montgomery
// multiplication needs, computed once.
//
// Build it with `montgomery`, keep it as long as the modulus lives, and
// pass it to `mont_mul`, `mont_sqr` and `mont_pow`.  It is a value: it
// can be stored in a struct, copied, and used from two places, and
// nothing about it changes after construction.
//
// The fields are public because a reader checking this implementation
// against Handbook of Applied Cryptography § 14.36 needs to see them,
// and because a test can then assert `r2` directly rather than only
// through a round trip.
pub struct BigMont
    // The modulus.  Odd, and that is the requirement `montgomery`
    // enforces.
    modulus: BigNat
    // How many limbs the modulus occupies.  `R` is `2^(limbs * 28)`.
    limbs: Int
    // `-n^-1 mod 2^28`: one limb, the multiplier the reduction step
    // uses to clear the low limb.
    n_prime: Int
    // `R^2 mod n`, the constant that converts a value INTO Montgomery
    // form with one multiplication.
    r2: BigNat
    // `R mod n`, which is Montgomery form's representation of 1 and the
    // accumulator every exponentiation ladder starts from.
    one: BigNat

// The Montgomery context for an odd modulus.
//
// `BigEvenModulus` for an even one, and `BigZeroModulus` for zero.  The
// requirement is not a limitation in practice: an RSA modulus is a
// product of two odd primes, a curve's field prime is odd, and a group
// order is odd — every modulus the crypto tier has.  A caller with an
// even modulus uses `pow`, which divides and has no such requirement.
//
// ```novo
// use bignum
// use bigmod
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match bignum.nat_from_int(11)
//         Ok(n) =>
//             match bigmod.montgomery(n)
//                 Ok(m) => println("${m.limbs}")   // 1
//                 Err(e) => println(e.message())
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn montgomery(n: BigNat) -> Result<BigMont, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.montgomery")

// A reduced value converted INTO Montgomery form: `a * R mod n`.
//
// One `mont_mul` against `r2`, which is what `r2` is for.
pub fn to_mont(m: BigMont, a: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.to_mont")

// A Montgomery-form value converted back: `a * R^-1 mod n`.
//
// One `mont_mul` against 1.
pub fn from_mont(m: BigMont, a: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.from_mont")

// The Montgomery product: `a * b * R^-1 mod n`, for two values already
// in Montgomery form.
//
// Interleaved multiply-and-reduce (HAC § 14.36): one limb of the
// product at a time, each followed by the reduction step that clears
// the low limb, so no full-width intermediate is ever held.  The result
// is in Montgomery form, which is what makes the form worth entering —
// a chain of these never leaves it.
//
// VARIABLE TIME only in the conditional final subtraction, which every
// textbook Montgomery multiplication has and which is a known leak of
// one bit per operation.  `mont_pow_ct` is written so that subtraction
// is performed unconditionally and its result selected, and that is the
// difference between the two exponentiations.
pub fn mont_mul(m: BigMont, a: BigNat, b: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.mont_mul")

// The Montgomery square: `a * a * R^-1 mod n`.
//
// Beside `mont_mul` for the same reason `bignum.nat_sqr` is beside
// `nat_mul` — an exponentiation squares once per bit and multiplies
// only for the set ones, so this is the hot call and it has a cheaper
// form.
pub fn mont_sqr(m: BigMont, a: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.mont_sqr")

// `base^exponent mod n`, through Montgomery form, with the conversions
// at each end.
//
// The caller passes and receives ORDINARY reduced values: the entry
// into Montgomery form and the exit are inside, because a caller who
// had to do them would eventually do one of them twice.  A caller
// already holding Montgomery-form values uses `mont_mul` and
// `mont_sqr` directly.
//
// VARIABLE TIME in the exponent, like `pow`.
pub fn mont_pow(m: BigMont, base: BigNat, exponent: BigNat) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.mont_pow")

// `base^exponent mod n` through Montgomery form, in CONSTANT TIME with
// respect to the exponent.
//
// The fixed ladder of `pow_ct` over the Montgomery operations, with the
// conditional final subtraction of `mont_mul` performed
// unconditionally and selected.  This is the call an RSA private
// operation and a scalar multiplication both want: the fastest
// exponentiation this package has AND the one whose operation sequence
// does not depend on the secret.
//
// `width` is the exponent's declared width in bits, for the reason
// `pow_ct` gives.
pub fn mont_pow_ct(m: BigMont, base: BigNat, exponent: BigNat,
                   width: Int) -> Result<BigNat, BigFault> []
    todo("bigint-nv.bigmod.mont_pow_ct")
